      "timestamp_minutes": 10.0,
      "type": "reassess",
      "args": {},
      "justification": "\u25cb OPTIONAL but clinically indicated - Given documented inhalation injury comorbidity, formal assessment of inhalation injury signs (carbonaceous sputum, singed nasal hairs, hoarseness, stridor) should be performed immediately alongside airway assessment to guide management decisions."
    },
    {
      "action_id": "assess_burn_depth",
      "timestamp_minutes": 15.0,
      "type": "reassess",
      "args": {},
      "justification": "Mandatory action: Assessing burn depth is essential to classify the severity of burns (superficial, partial thickness, full thickness) and guide treatment decisions including fluid resuscitation and surgical planning."
    },
    {
      "action_id": "estimate_tbsa",
      "timestamp_minutes": 20.0,
      "type": "reassess",
      "args": {},
      "justification": "Mandatory action: Estimating total body surface area (TBSA) affected by burns is critical for calculating fluid resuscitation requirements using the Parkland formula and determining burn center referral criteria."
    },
    {
      "action_id": "obtain_patient_weight",
      "timestamp_minutes": 25.0,
      "type": "reassess",
      "args": {},
      "justification": "Mandatory action: Patient weight is required to calculate the Parkland formula for IV fluid resuscitation (4 mL/kg/%TBSA burned in first 24 hours)."
    },
    {
      "action_id": "establish_iv_access",
      "timestamp_minutes": 30.0,
      "type": "reassess",
      "args": {},
      "justification": "MANDATORY action - large bore IV access is essential for fluid resuscitation in burn patients, especially with inhalation injury. Must be completed within 60-minute deadline."
    },
    {
      "action_id": "calculate_parkland_formula",
      "timestamp_minutes": 35.0,
      "type": "reassess",
      "args": {},
      "justification": "After establishing IV access, Parkland formula calculation is needed to determine fluid resuscitation volumes based on TBSA (already estimated) and patient weight (already obtained). Critical for burn management."
    },
    {
      "action_id": "assess_burn_center_referral_criteria",
      "timestamp_minutes": 40.0,
      "type": "reassess",
      "args": {},
      "justification": "Patient has inhalation injury which is a primary criterion for burn center referral per ABA guidelines. Must assess referral criteria to ensure appropriate level of care."
    },
    {
